body:
The problem may be due to #60177 dropping special checks for in-place operations when porting eq (and other comparison operations) to structured cores.

```
// To avoid overflow during type promotion we will check that both dtypes of self and other are same
template <typename OutImpl>
Tensor& comparison_op_(Tensor& self, const Tensor& other, OutImpl& out_impl) {
  TORCH_CHECK(self.dtype() == other.dtype(),
              "Expected object of scalar type ", self.dtype(), " but got scalar type ",
              other.dtype(), " for argument 'other'");
  return out_impl(self, self, other);
}

Tensor& eq_(Tensor& self, const Tensor& other) { return comparison_op_(self, other, static_cast<OutFunc>(at::eq_out)); }
```

Question: How do I perform special checks for in-place operations in the structured core?

Apparently, that check was introduced when porting `lt` to ATen, in #25998. Here is the reason given in that PR:

> **`a.lt_(Tensor b)`**
Expects that a and b has same dtype, otherwise it's possible to get an overflow(Example: 'a' is uint8, 'b' is float32. 'a' will be promoted to float32 and the result will be also float32. Then it will be casted back to uint8 so potential for overflow). Will not compute common dtype. Result will have type of a.

Maybe I'm missing something, but I can't see how that's the case. Both CPU and CUDA had very similar implementations, differing only in which `TensorIterator` consumer function was used: `cpu_kernel` and `gpu_kernel_with_scalars`, respectively. For example, the relevant part of [the CPU kernel](https://github.com/ifedan/pytorch/blob/2fed6b12dfbb7fa3f0a5ab540c8de665301145ca/aten/src/ATen/native/cpu/BinaryOpsKernel.cpp#L106-L122) follows:

```c++
AT_DISPATCH_ALL_TYPES_AND(kBFloat16, iter.dtype(), "lt_cpu", [&]() {
  cpu_kernel(iter,
    [=](scalar_t a, scalar_t b) -> scalar_t {
      return a < b;
    });
});
```

Note that the lambda function used return an implicit conversion of `bool` (the return type of the `<` operator) to `scalar_t`. As far as I understand, in that case, the result will always be either 0 or 1 of the return type. I can't really see how that could cause an overflow.

That said, I may be missing something. Any thoughts?
@ezyang @ifedan @zou3519
